in](https://accounts.x.ai/check-login?redirect=grokipedia-com&return_to=%2Fpage%2Fthe_rector_of_justin)\n\n\n\nFact-checked by Grok 6 months ago\n\n# The Rector of Justin\n\n*The 
Rector of Justin* is a 1964 psychological novel by American author Louis Auchincloss, chronicling the life and character of Reverend Frank Prescott, the founder and long-serving 
rector of Justin Martyr, a fictional elite Episcopal boys' preparatory school in rural New England.[[[1]](#ref-1)](#ref-1)[[[2]](#ref-2)](#ref-2) The narrative unfolds through the 
retrospective accounts of six narrators—including former students, colleagues, and family members—who offer contrasting perspectives on Prescott's authoritarian leadership, personal 
hypocrisies, and unyielding commitment to classical education and Christian moral rigor, revealing a man both inspiring and flawed.[[[3]](#ref-3)](#ref-3) Auchincloss, drawing from 
his own experiences at Groton School and his background in Manhattan's upper echelons, uses the novel to probe themes of power, faith, and institutional tradition amid 
mid-20th-century social shifts, portraying Prescott as a domineering figure whose ideals clash with evolving societal norms and personal scandals.[[[4]](#ref-4)](#ref-4) Regarded as 
one of Auchincloss's finest works, it has been lauded for its incisive character study and elegant prose, with critics highlighting Prescott as a complex archetype of American elite 
authority—capable of pettiness and self-dramatization yet driven by genuine conviction. The book received acclaim upon release, including praise from *The New Yorker* for its \"poise 
and taste and intelligence\";[[[5]](#ref-5)](#ref-5) in a 2008 *Washington Post* review, Jonathan Yardley described Prescott as \"one of the great characters in American 
fiction.\"[[[6]](#ref-6)](#ref-6)\n\n## Plot Synopsis\n\n### Overall Narrative\n\n*The Rector of Justin*, published in 1964, chronicles the life and legacy of Reverend Doctor Francis 
Prescott, the founder and longtime rector of Justin Martyr, an elite Episcopal boarding school for boys in New England. Born in 1860 and orphaned early after his father's death in the
Civil War, Prescott was educated at Oxford University, where he absorbed ideals of classical British education that he later sought to adapt for shaping the sons of America's upper 
class into moral and intellectual leaders. The novel's narrative spans Prescott's final years, beginning in autumn 1939 when he is 80 years old, and unfolds through a composite of 
personal accounts rather than linear chronology, emphasizing the complexities of his character and achievements.[[[7]](#ref-7)](#ref-7)[[[8]](#ref-8)](#ref-8) The story is primarily 
framed by the journal of Brian Aspinwall, a young and initially timid English instructor newly arrived at the school, who becomes captivated by Prescott's commanding presence and the 
institution's traditions. Aspinwall's observations during Prescott's last two years as rector—marked by strict discipline, loyalty to alma mater principles, and a belief in the divine
mission of elite education—evolve into an effort to compile a biography of the headmaster. This endeavor incorporates materials from five other key figures: Horace Havistock, 
Prescott's Oxford contemporary; David Griscam, a lawyer, early student, and school board chairman; Jules Griscam, David's son whose critical perspective stems from personal tragedy; 
Cordelia Prescott, the rector's youngest daughter harboring family grievances; and Charley Strong, a favored former pupil entangled in Prescott's personal sphere. These diverse 
viewpoints—drawn from diaries, partial memoirs, letters, and interview notes—reveal Prescott's sacrifices, including forgoing personal ambitions for ministry and school-building, as 
well as emerging tensions in his faith, family relations, and leadership style.[[[7]](#ref-7)](#ref…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/literature-and-writing/rector-justin-louis-auchincloss", "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss | Literature and Writing | 
Research Starters | EBSCO Research", "content": "The story follows Brian Aspinwall, an English teacher and Columbia and Oxford alumnus, who grapples with personal dilemmas as World 
War II looms.", "score": 0.6533265, "raw_content": "RESEARCH STARTER\n\n# The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss\n\n\"The Rector of Justin\" is a novel by Louis Auchincloss, set 
against the backdrop of a prestigious boys' school in rural Massachusetts, Justin Martyr, during the late 1930s. The story follows Brian Aspinwall, an English teacher and Columbia and
Oxford alumnus, who grapples with personal dilemmas as World War II looms. While he contemplates returning to England to join the war effort, he also considers pursuing a calling in 
the clergy, despite his uncertainties about it.\n\nAspinwall's experiences at the school are intricately tied to the legacy of its venerable headmaster, Reverend Francis Prescott, 
who, at eighty years old, still exerts considerable influence. Through Aspinwall, readers are introduced to a variety of characters, including Prescott's aristocratic wife, Harriet, 
whose insights into literature and life shape Aspinwall's understanding of his role. The narrative weaves a complex tapestry of relationships and memories, as Aspinwall collects 
impressions of Prescott from those who knew him, revealing the headmaster's multifaceted character as a minister, educator, and family man. The interplay of personal ambition, duty, 
and the moral complexities of education and leadership underpins the themes of Auchincloss's work, making it a poignant exploration of human experience and societal values in a 
changing world.\n\nAuthored By: Silet, Charles L. P. 1 of 2\n\nPublished In: 2022 2 of 2\n\n## On This Page\n\n## Full Article\n\n### The Rector of Justin by Louis 
Auchincloss\n\n**First published:** 1964\n\n**Type of work:** Novel\n\n**Type of plot:** Narrative and novel of manners\n\n**Time of plot:** 1879-1947\n\n**Locale:** Massachusetts and
New York City\n\n### Principal characters\n\n### The Story:\n\nBrian Aspinwall, a graduate of Columbia and Oxford (Christ Church), accepts a position in 1939 to teach English at 
Justin Martyr, a residential school for boys located in rural Massachusetts. The school is still under the tutelage of its founder and headmaster, the Reverend Francis Prescott, who 
is, at the age of eighty, still a force to be reckoned with. Aspinwall is torn between returning to England to be part of the war raging in Europe and staying in the United States to 
study for the clergy, a calling he expresses some doubts about.\n\nAlthough Aspinwall starts off rocky at Justin Martyr by not adequately disciplining his charges, the Reverend 
Prescott helps him out by demonstrating the proper way to dispense demerits and otherwise intimidate the boys in his house. He soon becomes a favorite of Mrs. Prescott—Harriet 
Winslow—the head’s aristocratic wife, who is dying; she requests that Aspinwall read to her Henry James, an author not appreciated by her husband. Harriet, a grandniece of Ralph Waldo
Emerson, the New England Transcendental philosopher, apparently was of great help to her husband during the school’s early years. After her death, Aspinwall is made assistant to the 
headmaster to lessen his administrative duties as he enters the final year of his headmastership. It is in this capacity that Aspinwall decides to construct a biographical portrait of
Prescott by collecting impressions of him from those who have known him through the years.\n\nThe first set of impressions comes from Horace Havistock’s manuscript for his unfinished 
work “The Art of Friendship,” and it provides the earliest memories of Prescott, who was Havistock’s boyhood friend. Havistock and Prescott met at the residential boy’s school St. 
Andrews in Dublin, New Hampshire, in 1876, and Prescott provided the less athletic and more effete Havistock with not only friendship but also protection from the bullying of the 
other boys. Later, Prescott founded his own school and based it on his experiences at St. Andrews, including his interactions with its headmaster, Dr. Howell. Prescott’s Justin Martyr
school became totally unlike St. Andrews, a place he abhorred.\n\nAfter attending Balliol College, Oxford, Prescott goes to wo…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
