    "raw_content_or_summary": "Raw content excerpt (key portions):\n\n\"The Rector of Justin is a 1964 psychological novel by American author Louis Auchincloss, chronicling the life 
and character of Reverend Frank Prescott, the founder and long-serving rector of Justin Martyr, a fictional elite Episcopal boys' preparatory school in rural New England. The 
narrative unfolds through the retrospective accounts of six narrators—including former students, colleagues, and family members—who offer contrasting perspectives on Prescott's 
authoritarian leadership, personal hypocrisies, and unyielding commitment to classical education and Christian moral rigor, revealing a man both inspiring and flawed. Auchincloss, 
drawing from his own experiences at Groton School and his background in Manhattan's upper echelons, uses the novel to probe themes of power, faith, and institutional tradition amid 
mid-20th-century social shifts, portraying Prescott as a domineering figure whose ideals clash with evolving societal norms and personal scandals. Regarded as one of Auchincloss's 
finest works, it has been lauded for its incisive character study and elegant prose, with critics highlighting Prescott as a complex archetype of American elite authority—capable of 
pettiness and self-dramatization yet driven by genuine conviction.\n\n## Plot Synopsis\n\n### Overall Narrative\n\nThe Rector of Justin, published in 1964, chronicles the life and 
legacy of Reverend Doctor Francis Prescott, the founder and longtime rector of Justin Martyr, an elite Episcopal boarding school for boys in New England. Born in 1860 and orphaned 
early after his father's death in the Civil War, Prescott was educated at Oxford University, where he absorbed ideals of classical British education that he later sought to adapt for 
shaping the sons of America's upper class into moral and intellectual leaders. The novel's narrative spans Prescott's final years, beginning in autumn 1939 when he is 80 years old, 
and unfolds through a composite of personal accounts rather than linear chronology, emphasizing the complexities of his character and achievements. The story is primarily framed by 
the journal of Brian Aspinwall, a young and initially timid English instructor newly arrived at the school, who becomes captivated by Prescott's commanding presence and the 
institution's traditions. Aspinwall's observations during Prescott's last two years as rector—marked by strict discipline, loyalty to alma mater principles, and a belief in the divine
mission of elite education—evolve into an effort to compile a biography of the headmaster. This endeavor incorporates materials from five other key figures: Horace Havistock, 
Prescott's Oxford contemporary; David Griscam, a lawyer, early student, and school board chairman; Jules Griscam, David's son whose critical perspective stems from personal tragedy; 
Cordelia Prescott, the rector's youngest daughter harboring family grievances; and Charley Strong, a favored former pupil entangled in Prescott's personal sphere. These diverse 
viewpoints—drawn from diaries, partial memoirs, letters, and interview notes—reveal Prescott's sacrifices ... as well as emerging tensions in his faith, family relations, and 
leadership style.\" \n\n(Article continues with more detailed plot events and thematic discussion; only the non-Wikipedia, plot-, character-, and setting-relevant portion is excerpted
here.)"
