[tools:b9f57cc7-c377-2034-5beb-5a96b96a54d1] [AIMessage] {
  "sources": [
    {
      "local_ref": "s1",
      "url": "https://grokipedia.com/page/the_rector_of_justin",
      "title": "The Rector of Justin — Grokipedia",
      "source_type": "other"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "s2",
      "url": "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/literature-and-writing/rector-justin-louis-auchincloss",
      "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss | Literature and Writing | Research Starters | EBSCO Research",
      "source_type": "other"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "s3",
      "url": "http://www.dpughphoto.com/realblog_13_rector_of_justin.html",
      "title": "The Rector of Justin (1964), by Louis Auchincloss: An Analysis",
      "source_type": "blog"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "s4",
      "url": "https://www.britannica.com/topic/The-Rector-of-Justin",
      "title": "The Rector of Justin | novel by Auchincloss",
      "source_type": "other"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "s5",
      "url": "https://mysteriumconiunctionis.com/2025/07/20/the-rector-of-justin/",
      "title": "The Rector of Justin (review post at Mysterium Coniunctionis)",
      "source_type": "blog"
    }
  ],
  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Reference-style article describing Louis Auchincloss’s 1964 novel \"The Rector of Justin.\" It presents the work as a psychological novel centered on 
Reverend Frank (Francis) Prescott, founder and long‑serving rector of Justin Martyr, a fictional elite Episcopal boys’ preparatory/boarding school in rural New England. The article 
emphasizes the multi‑narrator structure: six narrators, including former students, colleagues, and family members, whose retrospective accounts form a composite portrait of Prescott 
during his final years, beginning in autumn 1939 when he is 80. It sketches Prescott’s backstory (born 1860, father killed in the U.S. Civil War, educated at Oxford) and his aim to 
adapt classical British education to shape upper‑class American boys. The summary identifies Brian Aspinwall, a young, initially timid English instructor, as the primary framing 
narrator whose journal records Prescott’s last two years and who begins compiling a biography using materials from five other figures: Horace Havistock, David Griscam, Jules Griscam, 
Cordelia Prescott, and Charley Strong. These materials—diaries, memoirs, letters, interview notes—reveal Prescott’s authoritarian leadership, religious and educational ideals, family 
tensions, personal sacrifices, hypocrisies, and conflicts with changing mid‑20th‑century social norms. The article also notes that critics regard the novel as one of Auchincloss’s 
finest works and highlight Prescott as a complex archetype of American elite authority.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Educational \"research starter\" article on \"The Rector of Justin\" that combines bibliographic data, plot summary, and character and setting notes. It 
states that the novel, first published in 1964, is a narrative/novel of manners whose plot time spans 1879–1947 and whose locales are Massachusetts and New York City. The main 
present‑time action occurs in the late 1930s at Justin Martyr, a prestigious residential boys’ school in rural Massachusetts, as World War II approaches. The article presents Brian 
Aspinwall, an English teacher and graduate of Columbia and Oxford (Christ Church), as the central character, recruited in 1939 to teach at Justin Martyr. It describes his internal 
conflict between returning to England to join the war and remaining in the United States to pursue the clergy. Reverend Francis Prescott, the eighty‑year‑old founder and headmaster, 
is portrayed as still powerful and closely involved in school discipline, showing Aspinwall how to impose demerits and intimidate students. Prescott’s aristocratic wife, Harriet 
Winslow, grandniece of Ralph Waldo Emerson, is depicted as intellectually influential, requesting that Aspinwall read Henry James to her and having helped her husband during the 
school’s early years. After her death, Aspinwall becomes Prescott’s assistant and decides to construct a biographical portrait of Prescott by collecting impressions from people who 
knew him. The article notes that the first such impressions come from Horace Havistock’s unfinished manuscript \"The Art of Friendship,\" which contains early memories of Prescott 
from their time at St. Andrews, a residential boys’ school in Dublin, New Hampshire, in the 1870s. Prescott protected the less athletic Havistock from bullying and later founded 
Justin Martyr based on—but ultimately in reaction against—his experiences at St. Andrews. The piece hints at further plot developments and elaboration of relationships not included in
the excerpt.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Personal blog review and analytical essay on \"The Rector of Justin.\" It characterizes the novel as a well‑paced, articulate story that gradually 
introduces characters and problems whose resolutions illuminate the main figures. The blogger argues that the book also functions as a kind of mystery story: Brian Aspinwall, a new 
English literature instructor at Justin Martyr and an aspiring minister, serves as an amateur detective puzzling over the extraordinary success of the Episcopal boys’ school and 
leaving clues about how it was achieved. The post summarizes Prescott’s life as the \"bare‑bones\" plot: Prescott (Francis Prescott, D.D., known as Frank among intimates), born around
1857, is an introverted, high‑achieving boy who befriends Horace Havistock at a private boys’ school run by a religious fanatic. He becomes religious early, studies divinity at 
Harvard and Oxford, spends time in business in New York City, falls in love with an unsuitable woman, then breaks with her, marries Harriet (a more prudent choice), and founds Justin 
Martyr, modeled closely on his earlier school experience. He runs the school for almost sixty years with a harshly doctrinaire and authoritarian religious style, growing increasingly 
out of touch with the secular orientation of the board and disenchantment with his methods. He has three daughters, acknowledges that he did not love them enough, regrets lacking a 
son; the first two daughters settle into domestic life, while the third remains a thorn in his side. Harriet develops a habit of befriending new instructors. Prescott’s relationship 
