Auchincloss.\n\nThis summary is AI-generated. [Learn More »](https://www.newyorker.com/about/faq#additional-offerings)\n\nBy [Whitney Balliett](/contributors/whitney-balliett)\n\nFOR 
all its conventionality, its quiet stylistic grace, and its orotund moral sense, Louis Au-chincloss’s new nov-el, “The Rector of Justin” (Houghton Mifflin), is a daring and ambitious 
book. At first glance, the chances it takes are small and safe. It deals principally with an eminent, long-lived (1860-1946) New England educator, it frets over such matters as 
honesty and goodness and godliness, and it moves steadily among the rich and/or wellborn. Yet it is almost always on thin ice. For it is an attempt at a full-length portrait of a 
great man—which in fiction is an almost impossible task, generally resulting in a series of blinding closeups and fervent eulogies. It is also a novel about a school—an equally 
difficult subject, and one that has driven novelists to either brutal exorcising or genteel watercolors. And it is centered on the seemingly insouciant rich and highborn, who have 
repeatedly caused writers to deify them (Henry James), seem proprietary about them (Edith Wharton), fawn over them (Dickens, Scott Fitzgerald), or simply lacerate them (Evelyn Waugh, 
Ronald Fir-bank). It does, by the way, appear odd, when one considers their extraordinary impress on affairs in general, that the rich are no longer fashionable subjects for the 
novel. But Au-chincloss meets nearly all these challenges, and though he himself could be the protagonist (old New York family, Groton, Yale, University of Virginia, law practice) of 
the very novel he has written, he views his background with steady wit and irony.\n\nThe hero of “The Rector of Justin s Francis Prescott, a New England aristocrat and a distant 
cousin of the great historian. His father is killed in the Civil War and his mother dies of grief soon after, so he is raised by aunts and uncles. By the time he reaches Oxford, by 
way of a New Hampshire preparatory school, his character is firmly set. He is a short, broad, strong-faced boy who excels at both athletics and studies. He is a natural leader and he 
is deeply religious. He has already had a vision of what his lifework will be—to found a religious yet liberal New England prep school. This vision, though, melts away while he is at 
college, and then, tormented by a microscopic reading of the New Testament, he loses his religion. He takes a job with the New York Central in New York and falls in love with an 
outspoken Western beauty. He need only reach out to acquire a wealthy and socially flawless career. But one night his father appears to him—as a ghost or in a dream—and tells him that
he must seek out God. After soul-wrestling of a sort that would not be possible today, he gives up his job and the girl and goes to divinity school, and in 1886 he founds Justin 
Martyr, an Episcopal boys’ boarding school, in a small town thirty miles west of Boston. He marries a classic Boston girl (hawk-nosed, amusing, intelligent) and has three daughters. 
By the turn of the century, he has become the lion of New England headmasters. Like many great leaders and teachers, he is a consummate ham and a thorough cosmopolite. He reads and 
speaks Greek and Latin fluently, he knows his Henry James and his wines and his Europe. He is rather a wit, and his sermons and lectures are handsomely modulated. His religion 
contains him, but he is also a just and brilliant liberal. He is revered and feared by his students. His reputation overflows New England, and by the time the Second World War arrives
Justin Martyr is widely regarded as the finest school of its kind in the country. Though still at the height of his powers, Prescott retires, at the age of eighty-one, and five years 
