of Justin*** is a 1964 [psychological fiction](/wiki/Psychological_fiction \"Psychological fiction\") novel by [Louis Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss \"Louis Auchincloss\") about
the headmaster (or \"[rector](/wiki/Rector_(ecclesiastical) \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\") of a socially exclusive American [boarding school](/wiki/Boarding_school \"Boarding 
school\"). Over the decades, various narrators provide contrasting perspectives on rector Francis Prescott's charismatic personality and autocratic leadership style. Through the 
narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils that [White Anglo-Saxon Protestant](/wiki/White_Anglo-Saxon_Protestants \"White Anglo-Saxon Protestants\") society—of which 
Prescott is a reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values.\n\nThe novel was a commercial hit and was warmly received by most contemporary critics. It 
was a finalist for the [Pulitzer Prize for Fiction](/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize_for_Fiction \"Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\") and the [National Book Award for 
Fiction](/wiki/National_Book_Award_for_Fiction \"National Book Award for Fiction\"), losing to [Shirley Ann Grau](/wiki/Shirley_Ann_Grau \"Shirley Ann Grau\")'s *[The Keeper of the 
House](/wiki/The_Keepers_of_the_House \"The Keepers of the House\")* and [Saul Bellow](/wiki/Saul_Bellow \"Saul Bellow\")'s *[Herzog](/wiki/Herzog_(novel) \"Herzog (novel)\")*, 
respectively. Its loss to *Herzog* marked [American literary fiction](/wiki/American_literature#Post-World_War_II_fiction \"American literature\")'s transition from realist [society 
novels](/wiki/Society_novel \"Society novel\") about the [American upper class](/wiki/American_upper_class \"American upper class\") to poetic prose, [avant-garde 
style](/wiki/Avant-garde_literature \"Avant-garde literature\"), and culturally diverse protagonists.\n\nUpon its release, *[Harper's Magazine](/wiki/Harper%27s_Magazine \"Harper's 
Magazine\")* called the book \"almost certainly the finest work ever written about an American [preparatory school](/wiki/College-preparatory_school \"College-preparatory school\")\".
It is often considered Auchincloss' greatest novel.\n\n## Synopsis\n\nThe story is told through six narrators: [frame narrator](/wiki/Frame_story \"Frame story\") Brian Aspinwall, a 
teacher at Massachusetts [Episcopal](/wiki/Episcopal_Church_(United_States) \"Episcopal Church (United States)\") boarding school Justin Martyr (\"Justin\" for short; named for [the 
Christian figure](/wiki/Justin_Martyr \"Justin Martyr\")), who is asked to write a biography of its founder Francis Prescott; and writings and interviews from five people who knew 
him.\n\n### Brian Aspinwall\n\nIn 1939, Aspinwall begins teaching English at Justin. Prescott's contradi…(truncated)"}, {"url": 
