War II looms. While he", "score": 0.54956865, "raw_content": "RESEARCH STARTER\n\n# The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss\n\n\"The Rector of Justin\" is a novel by Louis 
Auchincloss, set against the backdrop of a prestigious boys' school in rural Massachusetts, Justin Martyr, during the late 1930s. The story follows Brian Aspinwall, an English teacher
and Columbia and Oxford alumnus, who grapples with personal dilemmas as World War II looms. While he contemplates returning to England to join the war effort, he also considers 
pursuing a calling in the clergy, despite his uncertainties about it.\n\nAspinwall's experiences at the school are intricately tied to the legacy of its venerable headmaster, Reverend
Francis Prescott, who, at eighty years old, still exerts considerable influence. Through Aspinwall, readers are introduced to a variety of characters, including Prescott's 
aristocratic wife, Harriet, whose insights into literature and life shape Aspinwall's understanding of his role. The narrative weaves a complex tapestry of relationships and memories,
as Aspinwall collects impressions of Prescott from those who knew him, revealing the headmaster's multifaceted character as a minister, educator, and family man. The interplay of 
personal ambition, duty, and the moral complexities of education and leadership underpins the themes of Auchincloss's work, making it a poignant exploration of human experience and 
societal values in a changing world.\n\nAuthored By: Silet, Charles L. P. 1 of 2\n\nPublished In: 2022 2 of 2\n\n## On This Page\n\n## Full Article\n\n### The Rector of Justin by 
Louis Auchincloss\n\n**First published:** 1964\n\n**Type of work:** Novel\n\n**Type of plot:** Narrative and novel of manners\n\n**Time of plot:** 1879-1947\n\n**Locale:** 
Massachusetts and New York City\n\n### Principal characters\n\n### The Story:\n\nBrian Aspinwall, a graduate of Columbia and Oxford (Christ Church), accepts a position in 1939 to 
teach English at Justin Martyr, a residential school for boys located in rural Massachusetts. The school is still under the tutelage of its founder and headmaster, the Reverend 
Francis Prescott, who is, at the age of eighty, still a force to be reckoned with. Aspinwall is torn between returning to England to be part of the war raging in Europe and staying in
the United States to study for the clergy, a calling he expresses some doubts about.\n\nAlthough Aspinwall starts off rocky at Justin Martyr by not adequately disciplining his 
charges, the Reverend Prescott helps him out by demonstrating the proper way to dispense demerits and otherwise intimidate the boys in his house. He soon becomes a favorite of Mrs. 
Prescott—Harriet Winslow—the head’s aristocratic wife, who is dying; she requests that Aspinwall read to her Henry James, an author not appreciated by her husband. Harriet, a 
grandniece of Ralph Waldo Emerson, the New England Transcendental philosopher, apparently was of great help to her husband during the school’s early years. After her death, Aspinwall 
is made assistant to the headmaster to lessen his administrative duties as he enters the final year of his headmastership. It is in this capacity that Aspinwall decides to construct a
biographical portrait of Prescott by collecting impressions of him from those who have known him through the years.\n\nThe first set of impressions comes from Horace Havistock’s 
manuscript for his unfinished work “The Art of Friendship,” and it provides the earliest memories of Prescott, who was Havistock’s boyhood friend. Havistock and Prescott met at the 
residential boy’s school St. Andrews in Dublin, New Hampshire, in 1876, and Prescott provided the less athletic and more effete Havistock with not only friendship but also protection 
from the bullying of the other boys. Later, Prescott founded his own school and based it on his experiences at St. Andrews, including his interactions with its headmaster, Dr. Howell.
