plot summary setting characters Auchincloss", "importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260703163815472172_a30a6cae", "type": "Claim", "text": "\"The Rector of Justin\" is a 
psychological novel by American author Louis Auchincloss, first published in 1964, centered on Reverend Francis (Frank) Prescott and the elite Episcopal boys’ preparatory school 
Justin Martyr in rural New England.", "source_ids": ["20260703163814912466_e49dd751"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": "20260703163815568160_2431dd58", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
narrative of \"The Rector of Justin\" is structured through multiple retrospective narrators, with Brian Aspinwall’s journal providing the primary frame and supplemented by accounts 
from Horace Havistock, David Griscam, Jules Griscam, Cordelia Prescott, and Charley Strong.", "source_ids": ["20260703163814912466_e49dd751"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260703163815673140_548f7770", "type": "Claim", "text": "In the novel, Reverend Francis Prescott is depicted as the founder and long‑serving rector of Justin Martyr, whose 
authoritarian leadership, strict religious and educational ideals, and family relationships are scrutinized against changing mid‑20th‑century social norms.", "source_ids": 
["20260703163814912466_e49dd751"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": "20260703163815772352_249ab3f3", "type": "Claim", "text": "The main present‑time action of \"The Rector of Justin\" takes 
place in the late 1930s at Justin Martyr, a prestigious residential boys’ school in rural Massachusetts, with the overall plot time spanning 1879 to 1947 and locales including 
Massachusetts and New York City.", "source_ids": ["20260703163814963546_a069bb1f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": "20260703163815875257_4d8cc02d", "type": "Claim", "text": "Brian 
Aspinwall, an English teacher and graduate of Columbia and Oxford (Christ Church), is a principal character who arrives at Justin Martyr in 1939, wrestles with whether to return to 
England to join World War II or remain to pursue the clergy, and decides to construct a biographical portrait of Reverend Francis Prescott.", "source_ids": 
["20260703163814912466_e49dd751", "20260703163814963546_a069bb1f"], "citations": [6, 7]}, {"id": "20260703163815979333_0c48a79f", "type": "Claim", "text": "Harriet Winslow Prescott, 
Reverend Prescott’s aristocratic wife and grandniece of Ralph Waldo Emerson, plays a significant role in the novel as a literary and moral influence on Brian Aspinwall and as a 
supporter of her husband during Justin Martyr’s early years.", "source_ids": ["20260703163814963546_a069bb1f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": "20260703163816094146_1006c057", "type": 
"Claim", "text": "Horace Havistock’s unfinished manuscript \"The Art of Friendship\" within the novel provides early memories of Prescott from their time as schoolboys at St. Andrews 
in Dublin, New Hampshire, and helps trace Prescott’s development and his decision to found Justin Martyr.", "source_ids": ["20260703163814963546_a069bb1f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260703163816192692_057220c2", "type": "Claim", "text": "The novel portrays Francis Prescott as a high‑achieving, introverted man who becomes religious early, studies divinity at 
Harvard and Oxford, spends time in business in New York City, and eventually founds Justin Martyr modeled on a private boys’ school run by a religious fanatic.", "source_ids": 
["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": "20260703163816301636_2c685e74", "type": "Claim", "text": "Prescott runs Justin Martyr for almost sixty years with a 
harshly religiously doctrinaire and authoritarian style, becoming increasingly out of touch with the school’s secularizing board of trustees and facing growing disenchantment with his
methods.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": "20260703163816403692_82bd215c", "type": "Claim", "text": "In the novel’s portrayal, Prescott has
three daughters whom he admits he did not love enough, regrets not having a son, and experiences ongoing conflict with his third daughter, while his wife Harriet habitually befriends 
newly arrived instructors at the school.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": "20260703163816510573_535db8e1", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Prescott’s long and troubled relationship with trustee David Griscam and Griscam’s son Jules culminates in Prescott being persuaded by the board to retire at age eighty‑two, after 
which he briefly attempts to undermine his successor before recognizing that opinion has turned against him.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, 
{"id": "20260703163816618913_4949841a", "type": "Claim", "text": "Following retirement, Prescott becomes terminally ill about four years later and is depicted as vacillating between 
acceptance of his prognosis and fear of divine judgment, forming part of the novel’s concluding phase.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260703163816732564_892bfb1f", "type": "Claim", "text": "The story material about Prescott’s life and Justin Martyr is entrusted to Brian Aspinwall through accounts from Prescott 
and other figures with varying motivations, along with entries from Aspinwall’s own diary.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815007077_1f321691"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260703163816831686_0a8d0e5c", "type": "Claim", "text": "\"The Rector of Justin\" is recognized as a novel by Louis Auchincloss in standard reference sources such as Encyclopaedia 
Britannica.", "source_ids": ["20260703163815051229_3786caa4"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": "20260703163816929963_8ed1b6ba", "type": "Claim", "text": "In the novel, Brian Aspinwall is 
around twenty‑seven years old when he is suddenly recruited in 1939 to teach at Justin Martyr, a prestigious private boys’ school in New England, replacing a master who leaves for 
